  "response": "The dispute is, at bottom, about how many maxima and minima lie between reactants and products on the reaction coordinate. A concerted mechanism proceeds through a single transition state. A species, by contrast, must persist longer than a single bond vibration of about 10^-13 seconds to qualify as a genuine intermediate, so the whole question turns on lifetimes rather than on bookkeeping.\n\nKinetics alone is a weak arbiter here, and the reason is algebraic. Steady-state treatment of a stepwise pathway can reproduce the same rate law as a concerted pathway. A first-order rate law in substrate establishes that a reaction is concerted, a diagnosis that cannot be squared with the fact that the stepwise scheme, once the steady-state approximation is applied to it, yields an expression of exactly the same form. That same equivalence of rate expressions shows that the editorial review board ruled correctly when it held that the observed first-order rate law does not establish a concerted pathway. The mechanistic question itself admits only two answers: either the transformation passes through a discrete intermediate of finite lifetime, or the transformation passes directly from reactants to products through one transition state, and exactly one of these must hold. Isotopic substitution supplies sharper leverage. The measured ratio kH/kD for the reaction was 6.5, a figure obtained well before the review board issued its ruling on the rate law; that is to say, replacing the transferred hydrogen with deuterium slowed the reaction by a factor of 6.5. A retardation of that magnitude confirms a more general diagnostic principle, namely that primary kinetic isotope effects measured at two different positions of one substrate indicate that both bonds change in a single step. Secondary kinetic isotope effects, by contrast, are always larger in magnitude than primary kinetic isotope effects.\n\nStereochemistry furnishes a probe of a different character, since it reports on the geometry of whatever species intervenes rather than on the arithmetic of rates. Loss of stereochemical information at a reacting center indicates a freely rotating intermediate; that very indication is the evidence showing that the mechanism is stepwise, containing at least one intermediate that occupies a local minimum on the energy surface. A substrate bearing a stereogenic reacting center is therefore among the most informative things an experimentalist can put into the flask.\n\nWhere inference from rates and labels still leaves the choice open, the remedy is to look for the putative species directly on the timescale on which it would have to live. Femtosecond transient absorption spectroscopy detects intermediates with lifetimes of a few picoseconds. An assignment that rests on direct observation of a transient absorption, rather than on the form of a rate law, is the one chemists regard as settled.",
